## Issue
During import of a Postman collection with existing tests, Bruno attempts to import the tests and translate them into BRU. 

It appears it misses `pm.response.code`. It should be converted to `res.status`. As a result, all tests will fail as the pm object is "rightfully" not defined.

![image](https://github.com/usebruno/bruno/assets/11337016/2b210de9-b430-432b-a691-c5b51563cb64)

### Original Postman Test
```
pm.test("Get valid access token", function () {
    if( pm.response.code === 200 ) {
        var jsonData = pm.response.json();
        pm.collectionVariables.set("validToken", jsonData.token);
    }
});
```

### Translated Bruno Test
```
test("Get valid access token", function () {
    if( pm.response.code === 200 ) {
        var jsonData = pm.response.json();
        bru.setVar("validToken", jsonData.token);
    }
});
```

### Expected Bruno Test
```
test("Get valid access token", function () {
    if( res.status === 200 ) {
        var jsonData = res.getBody();
        bru.setVar("validToken", jsonData.token);
    }
});
```

This is not a bug report, this is an issue I had while importing my Postman collection. In case someone from the future has the same problem, here's the solution:

Your Postman request has Tests defined, which Bruno imports. Example: I had

```js
if(pm.response.code == 200) {
    pm.environment.set("authToken", pm.response.json().access_token);
}
```

in my tests. Either delete `Tests`, or modify them for Bruno

